import type { StudyTrack } from './quant'

/**
 * NeetCode's problem lists (neetcode.io/practice): the standard software
 * engineering interview drill, alongside the quant tracks in this app.
 *
 * Same rule as the rest of this file: we link to LeetCode, we don't
 * reproduce solutions. Every item is a real LeetCode problem, and item ids
 * double as `done` keys (see `AppContext`), so they are globally unique and
 * numbered once, forever - never renumber one, or you silently wipe that
 * problem's tick for every user who has ticked it.
 *
 * Provenance, because a stale or invented list here is worse than none:
 * - Blind 75 and NeetCode 150 membership, per-problem topic and difficulty
 *   come straight from NeetCode's own public repository
 *   (github.com/neetcode-gh/leetcode, .problemSiteData.json), which tags
 *   every problem it ships with `blind75` / `neetcode150` booleans. That is
 *   about as close to "ask NeetCode" as a scrape gets.
 * - NeetCode 250 has no such flag in that file, so it is built as the
 *   NeetCode 150 above plus the 100 additional problems found in a
 *   community mirror of the neetcode.io practice page. Every one of the 150
 *   shared problems was checked against the official file first - topic and
 *   category agree in all 150 cases, which is the reason the other 100 are
 *   trusted rather than dropped.
 * - Every single slug across all three lists (250 unique problems in total,
 *   since Blind 75 is a subset of NeetCode 150, which is a subset of
 *   NeetCode 250) was looked up individually against LeetCode's own GraphQL
 *   API on 2026-08-31, which returns null for a slug that does not exist.
 *   All 250 resolved, and the title/difficulty stored below are LeetCode's
 *   own canonical values from that lookup (not NeetCode's display copy,
 *   which capitalizes some titles differently), so a chip here always
 *   agrees with the LeetCode page it links to.
 */

